{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HQWBuQSrCY32sjiBX4g7gq","type":"tool_result","content":"<persisted-output>\nOutput too large (67.3KB). Full output saved to: /home/go/.claude/projects/-var-tmp-pose-work-go-pose-review-run-8b4b067166-worktree/[private-session]/tool-results/b9337l2v9.txt\n\nPreview (first 2KB):\ncommit aba3703b979379c1c4cf6815d3f875a0a94053db\nAuthor: Oseias Pereira <oseiaspereira88@gmail.com>\nDate:   Fri Nov 27 18:17:09 2026 -0300\n\n    fix(pose): return recorded continuations, keep a moved checkout working and recorded, name held paths\n    \n    POSE-Spec: pose-review-resume-residuals\n    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n    Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_018BdzfrSuCnWGHsxt9DVXwJ\n\ndiff --git a/POSE.md b/POSE.md\nindex 0635f3d5..25d520ec 100644\n--- a/POSE.md\n+++ b/POSE.md\n@@ -953,7 +953,7 @@ Reservations live in `.pose/review-attempts/`, final receipts link the run, and\n \n **Optional worktree runtime.** `pose runtime start` starts, detached, a runtime for this worktree; `attach` and `status` handshake with it, `stop` drains it (`--drain <d>`, default 5s) and says how many requests in flight finished and how many were abandoned, `restart` is stop then start, and `serve` runs it in the foreground (`--idle <d>`, default 30m, stops an idle runtime; 0 never does). It is opt-in: no other command starts it, and every command runs standalone whether it runs or not. The runtime is identified by the user, the canonical worktree (symlinks resolved) and the Git common directory, so two worktrees of one repository, or two users, never share one; the handshake compares the engine, build, protocol and schema as well and an attach from another engine is refused naming the field. Its endpoint is a Unix domain socket (Linux, macOS) of mode 0600 in a 0700 directory owned by the user (`$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pose`, else `pose-runtime-<uid>` under the temporary directory, or `POSE_RUNTIME_DIR`), whose peer the operating system identifies before a byte is read; a link, a loose mode, another owner or a file that is not a socket is refused, and a socket nobody answers on is replaced. There is no TCP and no token. Slice 1 hosts no domain operation: it answers handshake, status and stop, confers no authority, signature or presence, and every restart has\n...\n</persisted-output>","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"[private-session]","uuid":"fb79f12a-0a36-4027-a0ca-e103ef17cdee","timestamp":"2026-11-27T22:06:23.736Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"commit aba3703b979379c1c4cf6815d3f875a0a94053db\nAuthor: Oseias Pereira <oseiaspereira88@gmail.com>\nDate:   Fri Nov 27 18:17:09 2026 -0300\n\n    fix(pose): return recorded continuations, keep a moved checkout working and recorded, name held paths\n    \n    POSE-Spec: pose-review-resume-residuals\n    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>\n    Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_018BdzfrSuCnWGHsxt9DVXwJ\n\ndiff --git a/POSE.md b/POSE.md\nindex 0635f3d5..25d520ec 100644\n--- a/POSE.md\n+++ b/POSE.md\n@@ -953,7 +953,7 @@ Reservations live in `.pose/review-attempts/`, final receipts link the run, and\n \n **Optional worktree runtime.** `pose runtime start` starts, detached, a runtime for this worktree; `attach` and `status` handshake with it, `stop` drains it (`--drain <d>`, default 5s) and says how many requests in flight finished and how many were abandoned, `restart` is stop then start, and `serve` runs it in the foreground (`--idle <d>`, default 30m, stops an idle runtime; 0 never does). It is opt-in: no other command starts it, and every command runs standalone whether it runs or not. The runtime is identified by the user, the canonical worktree (symlinks resolved) and the Git common directory, so two worktrees of one repository, or two users, never share one; the handshake compares the engine, build, protocol and schema as well and an attach from another engine is refused naming the field. Its endpoint is a Unix domain socket (Linux, macOS) of mode 0600 in a 0700 directory owned by the user (`$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pose`, else `pose-runtime-<uid>` under the temporary directory, or `POSE_RUNTIME_DIR`), whose peer the operating system identifies before a byte is read; a link, a loose mode, another owner or a file that is not a socket is refused, and a socket nobody answers on is replaced. There is no TCP and no token. Slice 1 hosts no domain operation: it answers handshake, status and stop, confers no authority, signature or presence, and every restart has a new epoch, inheriting nothing. On Windows, which has no named-pipe implementation here, `status` reports `unsupported` and every command runs standalone. `status` exits 0 for running, standalone and unsupported and 1 for an incompatible runtime; `attach` also exits 1 when there is nothing to attach to. `--json` prints the document `pose.runtime.status/1`.\n \n-**Hosted checks and the resident Git memo.** `pose runtime run [--envelope] [--timeout <d>] <command> [args...]` runs `review-check`, `closeout-check`, `artifact-check`, `surface-check` or `roadmap-check` in the runtime of the worktree when one is running, compatible and willing, and standalone in this process otherwise; the exit code and the output are the command's, and `--envelope` prints one document (`pose.runtime.run/1`) with them and with `via` (`runtime` or `standalone`), the `fallback_reason`, the typed `outcome` (`completed`; `error` when the command ran and ended but its output (over 2 MiB) is not sent back, with the command's exit code; or `unknown` for a deadline or a lost answer), the epoch and the memo hits. The runtime refuses, before the command starts, one it does not host and a caller whose environment digest (the `POSE_`, `HARNE8_`, `GIT_`, `CLAUDE` and `CODEX` variables, `XDG_STATE_HOME`, `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` and `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`, the locale, `HOME`, `NO_COLOR` and the user) differs from its own, so the caller runs standalone; a command that reached the runtime and whose answer did not come back whole, or whose `--timeout` passed, may have run and is never repeated standalone unasked: it exits 1 and says so. The runtime runs hosted commands as the user that started it, in its worktree, with the output of a non-terminal, in the admission lanes described below; a command still waiting for its slot when its `--timeout` passes is refused, not started, so the caller runs it standalone. A runtime also keeps, between commands, the output of the Git reads that are pure functions of object ids or of the commit HEAD named when they ran, keyed by that commit and the shallow state (256 MiB, least recently used first; not for a linked worktree): the second `review-check` of this repository took about 10 s against about 25 s standalone, with the same output. The runtime admits hosted commands in two lanes, each with its own slots and a bounded queue: `short` (`surface-check`, about 10 ms on this repository) and `heavy` (the other hosted commands; `roadmap-check <slug>` takes minutes here), 2 and 1 slots by default and queues of 16 and 4; `pose runtime start|serve` take `--short-slots`, `--heavy-slots`, `--short-queue`, `--heavy-queue` and `--memo-mib` (default 256). A short query is not queued behind a heavy check, and a command that finds every slot of its lane busy and the queue full is refused before it waits and runs standalone. `status` and the handshake carry, per lane, `<lane>_slots`, `_queue_limit`, `_queued`, `_running`, `_admitted`, `_refused`, `_wait_ms_total` and `_wait_ms_max` in `metrics`. A measurement of the runtime on a real repository, with its thresholds fixed before the numbers, is `POSE_RUNTIME_MEASURE=<file> go test ./internal/cli -run TestMeasureTheRuntimeOnTheRealRepository`; its report is under `.pose/reports/`.\\n\\n**Review rounds.** The rounds of a scope are its sealed bundles that a completed delegated review concluded on, in the order those runs ended; `pose review verify`, `pose delegate ls` and `pose insights` show the round. From round 2, the generated brief (template `review-brief/2`; runs briefed with `review-brief/1` still verify) carries a `Previous round` section built from the ledger: every open finding with the identifier it keeps across rounds (`R<round>-<id>`), its severity, disposition and location, the previous run and its draft digest. The reviewer's draft is the fenced code block whose info string is `pose-draft`: JSON with `schema` `pose.review.draft/v1`, `decision` and `findings` (`id`, `severity` high|medium|low, `disposition` blocker|follow-up, `file`, `line`, `summary`), and from round 2 `prior_findings` (`id`, `status` fixed|partial|not-fixed|superseded, `evidence`) and `sweep`. A finding reported `partial` or `not-fixed` keeps its identifier in the next brief. A round-2-or-later draft that leaves an open finding unaccounted, calls one `fixed` without evidence or states no sweep is recorded with `draft_problems`, `wait` reports it as `invalid`, and it is never accepted as an approval. With `routing.rounds` `continue-thread` (the default) a round resumes the session the previous round ended with, through the agent's resume command; it opens a new session, recording why, when the agent or its vendor changes, the chain of drafts is broken, that session cannot be resumed, or the round reaches `routing.round_threshold` (5 by default), where `verify` also warns that the rounds are not converging. `cold-session` opens a new session every round. Each run records its `round` and `thread`; `pose insights` reports, per scope, findings per round, rounds to approval, and how prior findings were accounted for in continued and in cold rounds. A previous round whose record is not intact makes the brief refuse.\n+**Hosted checks and the resident Git memo.** `pose runtime run [--envelope] [--timeout <d>] <command> [args...]` runs `review-check`, `closeout-check`, `artifact-check`, `surface-check` or `roadmap-check` in the runtime of the worktree when one is running, compatible and willing, and standalone in this process otherwise; the exit code and the output are the command's, and `--envelope` prints one document (`pose.runtime.run/1`) with them and with `via` (`runtime` or `standalone`), the `fallback_reason`, the typed `outcome` (`completed`; `error` when the command ran and ended but its output (over 2 MiB) is not sent back, with the command's exit code; or `unknown` for a deadline or a lost answer), the epoch and the memo hits. The runtime refuses, before the command starts, one it does not host and a caller whose environment digest (the `POSE_`, `HARNE8_`, `GIT_`, `CLAUDE` and `CODEX` variables, `XDG_STATE_HOME`, `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` and `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`, the locale, `HOME`, `NO_COLOR` and the user) differs from its own, so the caller runs standalone; a command that reached the runtime and whose answer did not come back whole, or whose `--timeout` passed, may have run and is never repeated standalone unasked: it exits 1 and says so. The runtime runs hosted commands as the user that started it, in its worktree, with the output of a non-terminal, in the admission lanes described below; a command still waiting for its slot when its `--timeout` passes is refused, not started, so the caller runs it standalone. A runtime also keeps, between commands, the output of the Git reads that are pure functions of object ids or of the commit HEAD named when they ran, keyed by that commit and the shallow state (256 MiB, least recently used first; not for a linked worktree): the second `review-check` of this repository took about 10 s against about 25 s standalone, with the same output. The runtime admits hosted commands in two lanes, each with its own slots and a bounded queue: `short` (`surface-check`, about 10 ms on this repository) and `heavy` (the other hosted commands; `roadmap-check <slug>` takes minutes here), 2 and 1 slots by default and queues of 16 and 4; `pose runtime start|serve` take `--short-slots`, `--heavy-slots`, `--short-queue`, `--heavy-queue` and `--memo-mib` (default 256). A short query is not queued behind a heavy check, and a command that finds every slot of its lane busy and the queue full is refused before it waits and runs standalone. `status` and the handshake carry, per lane, `<lane>_slots`, `_queue_limit`, `_queued`, `_running`, `_admitted`, `_refused`, `_wait_ms_total` and `_wait_ms_max` in `metrics`. A measurement of the runtime on a real repository, with its thresholds fixed before the numbers, is `POSE_RUNTIME_MEASURE=<file> go test ./internal/cli -run TestMeasureTheRuntimeOnTheRealRepository`; its report is under `.pose/reports/`.\\n\\n**Review rounds.** The rounds of a scope are its sealed bundles that a completed delegated review concluded on, in the order those runs ended; `pose review verify`, `pose delegate ls` and `pose insights` show the round. From round 2, the generated brief (template `review-brief/2`; runs briefed with `review-brief/1` still verify) carries a `Previous round` section built from the ledger: every open finding with the identifier it keeps across rounds (`R<round>-<id>`), its severity, disposition and location, the previous run and its draft digest. The reviewer's draft is the fenced code block whose info string is `pose-draft`: JSON with `schema` `pose.review.draft/v1`, `decision` and `findings` (`id`, `severity` high|medium|low, `disposition` blocker|follow-up, `file`, `line`, `summary`), and from round 2 `prior_findings` (`id`, `status` fixed|partial|not-fixed|superseded, `evidence`) and `sweep`. A finding reported `partial` or `not-fixed` keeps its identifier in the next brief. A round-2-or-later draft that leaves an open finding unaccounted, calls one `fixed` without evidence or states no sweep is recorded with `draft_problems`, `wait` reports it as `invalid`, and it is never accepted as an approval. With `routing.rounds` `continue-thread` (the default) a round resumes the session the previous round ended with, through the agent's resume command; it opens a new session, recording why, when the agent or its vendor changes, the chain of drafts is broken, that session cannot be resumed, the previous round's checkout path is still there (a leftover or a live run), or the round reaches `routing.round_threshold` (5 by default), where `verify` also warns that the rounds are not converging. `cold-session` opens a new session every round. Each run records its `round` and `thread`; `pose insights` reports, per scope, findings per round, rounds to approval, and how prior findings were accounted for in continued and in cold rounds. A previous round whose record is not intact makes the brief refuse.\n \n **Delegated runs over MCP.** `pose_delegate_start` (review, adjudicate or smoke), `pose_delegate_status`, `pose_delegate_wait`, `pose_delegate_resume` and `pose_delegate_handoff` run `pose delegate … --json` in the project the server resolved for the call, so every plan, refusal and run is the CLI's: a start, resume or handoff is always detached and returns once the run has reserved its attempt (without `apply` it previews), a status never blocks, and a wait blocks at most its `timeout_seconds` (300 at most), returning `still-running` when the bound passes. Refusals carry the same `reason_code` as the CLI's error document (`rerun-without-reason`, `budget-exhausted`, `containment-incompatible`, `agent-unavailable`, `no-agent`), and results carry the normalized summary only: no transcript, prompt, session reference or environment value. `pose delegate resume` and `handoff` take `--detach` as the purposes do. A task is not started over MCP.\n \ndiff --git a/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md b/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\nindex 2068a227..b404d643 100644\n--- a/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n+++ b/locales/pt-BR/POSE.md\n@@ -948,7 +948,7 @@ Reservas ficam em `.pose/review-attempts/`, recibos finais ligam o run, e `.pose\n \n **Runtime opcional por worktree.** `pose runtime start` inicia, destacado, um runtime para este worktree; `attach` e `status` fazem o handshake com ele, `stop` o drena (`--drain <d>`, padrão 5s) e diz quantas requisições em voo terminaram e quantas foram abandonadas, `restart` é stop e depois start, e `serve` o roda em primeiro plano (`--idle <d>`, padrão 30m, para um runtime ocioso; 0 nunca para). É opt-in: nenhum outro comando o inicia, e todo comando roda standalone com ou sem ele. O runtime é identificado pelo usuário, pelo worktree canônico (links resolvidos) e pelo diretório comum do Git, de modo que dois worktrees de um repositório, ou dois usuários, nunca compartilham um; o handshake compara também engine, build, protocolo e schema, e um attach de outro engine é recusado nomeando o campo. O endpoint é um socket de domínio Unix (Linux, macOS) com modo 0600 num diretório 0700 do usuário (`$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/pose`, senão `pose-runtime-<uid>` sob o diretório temporário, ou `POSE_RUNTIME_DIR`), cujo par o sistema operacional identifica antes de se ler um byte; um link, um modo frouxo, outro dono ou um arquivo que não é socket são recusados, e um socket em que ninguém responde é substituído. Não há TCP nem token. A fatia 1 não hospeda nenhuma operação do domínio: responde handshake, status e stop, não confere autoridade, assinatura nem presença, e cada restart tem uma época nova, sem herdar nada. No Windows, que não tem implementação de named pipe aqui, `status` informa `unsupported` e todo comando roda standalone. `status` sai com 0 para running, standalone e unsupported e com 1 para um runtime incompatível; `attach` também sai com 1 quando não há o que anexar. `--json` imprime o documento `pose.runtime.status/1`.\n \n-**Checks hospedados e o memo residente do Git.** `pose runtime run [--envelope] [--timeout <d>] <comando> [args...]` roda `review-check`, `closeout-check`, `artifact-check`, `surface-check` ou `roadmap-check` no runtime do worktree quando há um rodando, compatível e disposto, e standalone neste processo caso contrário; o código de saída e a saída são os do comando, e `--envelope` imprime um documento (`pose.runtime.run/1`) com eles e com `via` (`runtime` ou `standalone`), o `fallback_reason`, o `outcome` tipado (`completed`; `error` quando o comando rodou e terminou mas a saída (acima de 2 MiB) não volta, com o código de saída do comando; ou `unknown` para um prazo ou uma resposta perdida), a época e os acertos do memo. O runtime recusa, antes de o comando começar, o que ele não hospeda e um chamador cujo digest de ambiente (as variáveis `POSE_`, `HARNE8_`, `GIT_`, `CLAUDE` e `CODEX`, `XDG_STATE_HOME`, `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` e `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`, o locale, `HOME`, `NO_COLOR` e o usuário) difere do dele, e o chamador roda standalone; um comando que chegou ao runtime e cuja resposta não voltou inteira, ou cujo `--timeout` passou, pode ter rodado e nunca é repetido standalone sem pedido: sai com 1 e diz isso. O runtime roda os comandos hospedados como o usuário que o iniciou, no seu worktree, com a saída de um não terminal, nas faixas de admissão descritas adiante; um comando que ainda espera o seu slot quando o `--timeout` passa é recusado, não iniciado, de modo que o chamador o roda standalone. Um runtime também guarda, entre comandos, a saída das leituras do Git que são funções puras de ids de objeto ou do commit que o HEAD nomeava quando rodaram, chaveada por esse commit e pelo estado raso (256 MiB, a menos recentemente usada primeiro; não para um worktree vinculado): o segundo `review-check` deste repositório levou cerca de 10 s contra cerca de 25 s standalone, com a mesma saída. O runtime admite os comandos hospedados em duas faixas, cada uma com seus slots e uma fila limitada: `short` (`surface-check`, cerca de 10 ms neste repositório) e `heavy` (os demais comandos hospedados; `roadmap-check <slug>` leva minutos aqui), 2 e 1 slots por padrão e filas de 16 e 4; `pose runtime start|serve` aceitam `--short-slots`, `--heavy-slots`, `--short-queue`, `--heavy-queue` e `--memo-mib` (padrão 256). Uma consulta curta não espera atrás de um check pesado, e um comando que encontra todos os slots da sua faixa ocupados e a fila cheia é recusado antes de esperar e roda standalone. `status` e o handshake trazem, por faixa, `<faixa>_slots`, `_queue_limit`, `_queued`, `_running`, `_admitted`, `_refused`, `_wait_ms_total` e `_wait_ms_max` em `metrics`. Uma medição do runtime num repositório real, com os limiares fixados antes dos números, é `POSE_RUNTIME_MEASURE=<arquivo> go test ./internal/cli -run TestMeasureTheRuntimeOnTheRealRepository`; o relatório fica em `.pose/reports/`.\\n\\n**Rodadas de review.** As rodadas de um escopo são os bundles selados em que uma review delegada concluída terminou, na ordem em que esses runs terminaram; `pose review verify`, `pose delegate ls` e `pose insights` mostram a rodada. A partir da rodada 2, o brief gerado (template `review-brief/2`; runs com `review-brief/1` continuam verificando) traz uma seção `Previous round` construída do ledger: cada finding aberto com o identificador que mantém entre rodadas (`R<rodada>-<id>`), sua severidade, disposição e local, o run anterior e o digest do seu draft. O draft do revisor é o bloco de código cercado cuja info string é `pose-draft`: JSON com `schema` `pose.review.draft/v1`, `decision` e `findings` (`id`, `severity` high|medium|low, `disposition` blocker|follow-up, `file`, `line`, `summary`), e a partir da rodada 2 `prior_findings` (`id`, `status` fixed|partial|not-fixed|superseded, `evidence`) e `sweep`. Um finding reportado `partial` ou `not-fixed` mantém seu identificador no brief seguinte. Um draft da rodada 2 em diante que deixa um finding aberto sem conta, marca um como `fixed` sem evidência ou não declara a varredura é registrado com `draft_problems`, o `wait` o reporta como `invalid`, e ele nunca é aceito como aprovação. Com `routing.rounds` `continue-thread` (o padrão), uma rodada retoma a sessão com que a rodada anterior terminou, pelo comando de resume do agente; ela abre uma sessão nova, registrando o motivo, quando o agente ou seu vendor muda, a cadeia de drafts está quebrada, essa sessão não pode ser retomada, ou a rodada atinge `routing.round_threshold` (5 por padrão), onde o `verify` também avisa que as rodadas não estão convergindo. `cold-session` abre uma sessão nova a cada rodada. Cada run registra sua `round` e sua `thread`; `pose insights` reporta, por escopo, findings por rodada, rodadas até a aprovação, e como os findings anteriores foram contabilizados em rodadas continuadas e frias. Uma rodada anterior cujo registro não está íntegro faz o brief recusar.\n+**Checks hospedados e o memo residente do Git.** `pose runtime run [--envelope] [--timeout <d>] <comando> [args...]` roda `review-check`, `closeout-check`, `artifact-check`, `surface-check` ou `roadmap-check` no runtime do worktree quando há um rodando, compatível e disposto, e standalone neste processo caso contrário; o código de saída e a saída são os do comando, e `--envelope` imprime um documento (`pose.runtime.run/1`) com eles e com `via` (`runtime` ou `standalone`), o `fallback_reason`, o `outcome` tipado (`completed`; `error` quando o comando rodou e terminou mas a saída (acima de 2 MiB) não volta, com o código de saída do comando; ou `unknown` para um prazo ou uma resposta perdida), a época e os acertos do memo. O runtime recusa, antes de o comando começar, o que ele não hospeda e um chamador cujo digest de ambiente (as variáveis `POSE_`, `HARNE8_`, `GIT_`, `CLAUDE` e `CODEX`, `XDG_STATE_HOME`, `XDG_CONFIG_HOME` e `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`, o locale, `HOME`, `NO_COLOR` e o usuário) difere do dele, e o chamador roda standalone; um comando que chegou ao runtime e cuja resposta não voltou inteira, ou cujo `--timeout` passou, pode ter rodado e nunca é repetido standalone sem pedido: sai com 1 e diz isso. O runtime roda os comandos hospedados como o usuário que o iniciou, no seu worktree, com a saída de um não terminal, nas faixas de admissão descritas adiante; um comando que ainda espera o seu slot quando o `--timeout` passa é recusado, não iniciado, de modo que o chamador o roda standalone. Um runtime também guarda, entre comandos, a saída das leituras do Git que são funções puras de ids de objeto ou do commit que o HEAD nomeava quando rodaram, chaveada por esse commit e pelo estado raso (256 MiB, a menos recentemente usada primeiro; não para um worktree vinculado): o segundo `review-check` deste repositório levou cerca de 10 s contra cerca de 25 s standalone, com a mesma saída. O runtime admite os comandos hospedados em duas faixas, cada uma com seus slots e uma fila limitada: `short` (`surface-check`, cerca de 10 ms neste repositório) e `heavy` (os demais comandos hospedados; `roadmap-check <slug>` leva minutos aqui), 2 e 1 slots por padrão e filas de 16 e 4; `pose runtime start|serve` aceitam `--short-slots`, `--heavy-slots`, `--short-queue`, `--heavy-queue` e `--memo-mib` (padrão 256). Uma consulta curta não espera atrás de um check pesado, e um comando que encontra todos os slots da sua faixa ocupados e a fila cheia é recusado antes de esperar e roda standalone. `status` e o handshake trazem, por faixa, `<faixa>_slots`, `_queue_limit`, `_queued`, `_running`, `_admitted`, `_refused`, `_wait_ms_total` e `_wait_ms_max` em `metrics`. Uma medição do runtime num repositório real, com os limiares fixados antes dos números, é `POSE_RUNTIME_MEASURE=<arquivo> go test ./internal/cli -run TestMeasureTheRuntimeOnTheRealRepository`; o relatório fica em `.pose/reports/`.\\n\\n**Rodadas de review.** As rodadas de um escopo são os bundles selados em que uma review delegada concluída terminou, na ordem em que esses runs terminaram; `pose review verify`, `pose delegate ls` e `pose insights` mostram a rodada. A partir da rodada 2, o brief gerado (template `review-brief/2`; runs com `review-brief/1` continuam verificando) traz uma seção `Previous round` construída do ledger: cada finding aberto com o identificador que mantém entre rodadas (`R<rodada>-<id>`), sua severidade, disposição e local, o run anterior e o digest do seu draft. O draft do revisor é o bloco de código cercado cuja info string é `pose-draft`: JSON com `schema` `pose.review.draft/v1`, `decision` e `findings` (`id`, `severity` high|medium|low, `disposition` blocker|follow-up, `file`, `line`, `summary`), e a partir da rodada 2 `prior_findings` (`id`, `status` fixed|partial|not-fixed|superseded, `evidence`) e `sweep`. Um finding reportado `partial` ou `not-fixed` mantém seu identificador no brief seguinte. Um draft da rodada 2 em diante que deixa um finding aberto sem conta, marca um como `fixed` sem evidência ou não declara a varredura é registrado com `draft_problems`, o `wait` o reporta como `invalid`, e ele nunca é aceito como aprovação. Com `routing.rounds` `continue-thread` (o padrão), uma rodada retoma a sessão com que a rodada anterior terminou, pelo comando de resume do agente; ela abre uma sessão nova, registrando o motivo, quando o agente ou seu vendor muda, a cadeia de drafts está quebrada, essa sessão não pode ser retomada, o caminho do checkout da rodada anterior ainda existe (uma sobra ou uma run viva), ou a rodada atinge `routing.round_threshold` (5 por padrão), onde o `verify` também avisa que as rodadas não estão convergindo. `cold-session` abre uma sessão nova a cada rodada. Cada run registra sua `round` e sua `thread`; `pose insights` reporta, por escopo, findings por rodada, rodadas até a aprovação, e como os findings anteriores foram contabilizados em rodadas continuadas e frias. Uma rodada anterior cujo registro não está íntegro faz o brief recusar.\n \n **Runs delegados pelo MCP.** `pose_delegate_start` (review, adjudicate ou smoke), `pose_delegate_status`, `pose_delegate_wait`, `pose_delegate_resume` e `pose_delegate_handoff` rodam `pose delegate … --json` no projeto que o servidor resolveu para a chamada, então todo plano, recusa e run é o da CLI: um start, resume ou handoff é sempre destacado e volta quando o run reservou sua tentativa (sem `apply` ele faz a prévia), um status nunca bloqueia, e um wait bloqueia no máximo seu `timeout_seconds` (300 no máximo), devolvendo `still-running` quando o limite passa. Recusas trazem o mesmo `reason_code` do documento de erro da CLI (`rerun-without-reason`, `budget-exhausted`, `containment-incompatible`, `agent-unavailable`, `no-agent`), e os resultados trazem só o resumo normalizado: nenhum transcript, prompt, referência de sessão ou valor de ambiente. `pose delegate resume` e `handoff` aceitam `--detach` como os propósitos. Uma task não é iniciada pelo MCP.\n \ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/delegate_rounds_test.go b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/delegate_rounds_test.go\nindex 7275c0d5..354e03f3 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/cli/delegate_rounds_test.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/cli/delegate_rounds_test.go\n@@ -7,6 +7,7 @@ import (\n \t\"path/filepath\"\n \t\"strings\"\n \t\"testing\"\n+\t\"time\"\n \n \tposemodel \"github.com/harne8/pose-mcp/internal/pose\"\n )\n@@ -266,4 +267,25 @@ func TestReviewRoundsKeepTheCheckoutPathOfTheirThread(t *testing.T) {\n \tif third.Status != posemodel.ReviewRunCompleted || third.Thread == nil || third.Thread.Mode != posemodel.ReviewThreadNew || !strings.Contains(third.Thread.Reason, \"still in use\") {\n \t\tt.Fatalf(\"a round continued into a held path: status=%s thread=%+v failure=%s\", third.Status, third.Thread, third.Failure)\n \t}\n+\t// Spec pose-review-resume-residuals R4: the reason names the held path.\n+\tif !strings.Contains(third.Thread.Reason, held) {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"the reason does not name the held path %s: %s\", held, third.Thread.Reason)\n+\t}\n+\t// R3: a path taken after the round is planned does not fail the run; the\n+\t// round works in its own directory and the run says the checkout moved.\n+\tnextReviewRound(t, root, \"fix B3\")\n+\tstore := posemodel.Store{Root: root}\n+\tplan, err := store.PlanReviewDispatch(\"spec:alpha\", \"review\", \"\", \"rounder\", \"next round\")\n+\tif err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t}\n+\tlate := filepath.Join(os.TempDir(), third.Safety.RuntimeID)\n+\tif err := os.Mkdir(late, 0o700); err != nil {\n+\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n+\t}\n+\tdefer os.Remove(late)\n+\tfourth, err := store.RunReviewDispatch(plan, time.Now)\n+\tif err != nil || fourth.Status != posemodel.ReviewRunCompleted || fourth.Safety == nil || !strings.Contains(strings.Join(fourth.Safety.Limitations, \";\"), \"checkout moved\") {\n+\t\tt.Fatalf(\"a path taken after the plan: %v status=%s limits=%v\", err, fourth.Status, fourth.Safety)\n+\t}\n }\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_handoff.go b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_handoff.go\nindex c157a8cb..96fda428 100644\n--- a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_handoff.go\n+++ b/pose-mcp/internal/pose/delegation_handoff.go\n@@ -11,6 +11,7 @@ package pose\n // as the handoff run's own decision; only the handoff run concludes.\n \n import (\n+\t\"context\"\n \t\"errors\"\n \t\"fmt\"\n \t\"path/filepath\"\n@@ -348,10 +349,16 @@ func (s Store) PlanReviewHandoff(id, agent string) (DispatchPlan, error) {\n \t\t// Another resume takes the directory just before its claim: a claim\n \t\t// that appears meanwhile makes this one pending (spec\n \t\t// pose-review-resume-runtime-dir-followups R1).\n-\t\tif pending := s.awaitContinuationClaim(*prior); pending != nil {\n+\t\texisting, pending := s.awaitContinuationClaim(context.Background(), *prior)\n+\t\tif pending != nil {\n \t\t\tplan.PendingContinuation = pending\n \t\t\treturn plan, nil\n \t\t}\n+\t\tif existing != nil {\n+\t\t\t// Another resume ended meanwhile: its record is the answer\n+\t\t\t// (spec pose-review-resume-residuals R1).\n+\t\t\treturn DispatchPlan{}, fmt.Errorf(\"pose: run %s was already continued by run %s\", prior.RunID, existing.RunID)\n+\t\t}\n \t\treturn DispatchPlan{}, e\n \t}\n \tcount, spent, limit, total, e := attemptContinuationBudget(runs, *prior, policy)\ndiff --git a/pose-mcp/internal/pose/review_dispat